From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Leaf Performance Engineering <perf@leafmc.one>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 00:00:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] Cache ClientboundSetEntityMotionPacket in ServerEntity

The entity tracker constructs `new ClientboundSetEntityMotionPacket(id, lastSentMovement)`
on every motion broadcast, even though the payload is fully derived from two stable
fields: `this.entity.getId()` (constant for the lifetime of the ServerEntity) and
`this.lastSentMovement` (a Vec3 reference that is only reassigned — never mutated —
when the entity's delta movement actually changes, per the SparklyPaper identity-check
optimization in patch 0101).

Because both inputs are stable between actual motion changes, the constructed packet
itself is identical across sends and can be cached and shared across tracking players.
Netty's pipeline is safe to receive the same packet instance on multiple channels —
`ClientboundSetEntityMotionPacket` stores only `int xa, ya, za` (packed from the Vec3
once in the constructor) and the entity id, both of which are immutable after
construction. No reference to the live Entity is retained.

This patch adds two fields to `ServerEntity` and a small lazy-rebuild helper:

  - `leaf$cachedMotionPacketVec` : the `Vec3` reference used to build the cached packet
  - `leaf$cachedMotionPacket`   : the cached packet itself
  - `leaf$getOrBuildMotionPacket()` : rebuilds the cached packet iff the source Vec3
    reference has changed (identity check), otherwise returns the cached instance

The helper is applied at both motion-broadcast call sites covered by the existing
`0272-Filter-ClientboundSetEntityMotionPacket` patch: the legacy tracker path
(`synchronizer.sendToTrackingPlayers(...)`) and the multithreaded tracker path
(`ctx.sendToTrackingPlayers(tracker, ...)`).

A new config flag `cache-entity-motion-packet` (under `optimization.reduce-packets`,
gated by the existing `ReduceUselessPackets` config module) controls the optimization
and defaults to `false`. The default is conservative because ProtocolLib intercepts
`Connection#send` and may mutate the packet object on interception — sharing a single
instance across players could then leak mutation. With the flag disabled, behavior
is byte-for-byte identical to the unpatched baseline.

When enabled, expected benefit on entity-dense workloads (mob farms, projectile
arrays, dense item drops) is a substantial reduction in `ClientboundSetEntityMotionPacket`
allocations on the tracker path: per-call allocation goes to zero once the cache is
warm, leaving only the rare rebuild when the entity actually changes motion. The
GC pressure reduction is most visible on P99 MSPT under high entity counts.

